    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy.jpg" alt="Wilfred the Hairy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wilfred the Hairy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rotlle-genealogic-guifre-I-de-barcelona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Catalonia grew out of the Marca Hispanica, the frontier counties that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> created south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> in 785 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 801. In the late ninth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilfred-the-hairy">Wifred the Hairy</a>, count of Barcelona, Girona, Urgell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a>, made his counties hereditary in his family and founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monastery-of-santa-maria-de-ripoll">Ripoll</a>, and his successors in Barcelona gradually gained precedence over the other counts. After al-Mansur sacked Barcelona in 985 and no Frankish help came, Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borrell-ii-of-barcelona">Borrell II</a> ceased to seek confirmation from the new Capetian kings, and the counties became independent in practice. Ramon Berenguer I extended Barcelona’s authority in the eleventh century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tortosa</a> in 1148 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a> in 1149, completing what became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-catalonia">New Catalonia</a>. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronila</a>, heiress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>, agreed in 1137, joined the two dynasties, and in 1162 their son Alfonso II inherited Barcelona and soon Aragon, so that the Catalan counties became one constituent of the Crown of Aragon. The term Catalonia appears in the early twelfth century, and the name Principality became usual in the fourteenth. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-corbeil-1258">Treaty of Corbeil</a> of 1258 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a> renounced his old claims to suzerainty over the Catalan counties.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/barcelona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barcelona.jpg" alt="Barcelona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_light_over_Barcelona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">M McBey, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Medieval Catalonia combined a mountainous, seigneurial countryside with dynamic coastal towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade, sending cloth, coral, saffron and other goods to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and importing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, grain and slaves. Its merchants were organised in consulates abroad and governed by maritime customs collected in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consulate-of-the-sea">Llibre del Consolat de Mar</a></em>, which became widely used in Mediterranean commercial law. The city government, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-cent">Consell de Cent</a>, dates from the 1260s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taula-de-canvi">Taula de Canvi</a> of 1401 was among the first public banks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Many peasants, the <em>remences</em>, were bound to their lords and subject to the so-called evil customs, the <em>mals usos</em>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 and later epidemics cut the population sharply, perhaps by half over the following century and a half, unsettled the economy and sharpened conflict between lords and peasants and between the urban factions of the Biga and the Busca. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-remences">War of the Remences</a> ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sentence-of-guadalupe">Sentence of Guadalupe</a> of 1486, which let peasants buy their freedom from the evil customs. Catalan culture flourished in Romanesque churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sant-climent-de-taull">Sant Climent de Taüll</a>, the Gothic basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-mar">Santa Maria del Mar</a>, the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-llull">Ramon Llull</a> and a body of royal chronicles in Catalan.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halmyros"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-halmyros.jpg" alt="Battle of Halmyros" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halmyros">Battle of Halmyros</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greece_in_1278.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cplakidas, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Principality’s wars were fought within the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, whose expansion depended heavily on Catalan ships, money and men. Catalan nobles and towns contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I</a>’s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1229, and the royal shipyards at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drassanes-reials-de-barcelona">Drassanes</a>, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> that made the Crown a leading naval power. The admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> won a series of victories against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a> and the French, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iii-of-france">Philip III of France</a> invaded Catalonia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aragonese-crusade">Aragonese Crusade</a> of 1285, the French army was forced to retreat after its fleet was destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-les-formigues">Battle of Les Formigues</a>. Light infantry known as almogavers fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, and as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a> they served the Byzantine emperor from 1303 before seizing the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halmyros">Battle of Halmyros</a> in 1311. At home the towns raised militias and the countryside a general levy, the <em>sometent</em>. Later wars were fought on Catalan soil. In the civil war of 1462–1472 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat</a> raised armies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-aragon">John II</a>, and in the Reapers’ War Catalan and French troops defeated a royal army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montjuic-1641">Battle of Montjuïc</a> in January 1641. The final defence came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>, when Barcelona withstood a siege of more than a year before falling to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-fitzjames-1st-duke-of-berwick">Duke of Berwick</a> on 11 September 1714.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona.jpg" alt="Usatges of Barcelona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona">Usatges of Barcelona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Usatici-Barchinone-usalia-Cathalonie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alfons II d'Aragó, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Catalonia was governed under its own laws and institutions, with the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, who was also king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, as its prince. Customary law was gathered in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usatges-of-barcelona">Usatges of Barcelona</a></em>, traditionally attributed to Ramon Berenguer I but compiled in the twelfth century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-courts">Catalan Corts</a>, the parliament of clergy, nobility and royal towns, took definite form under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III</a>, who in 1283 promised to summon them every year; legislation required the consent of king and Corts together, an early form of contractual government that Catalans called pactism. In 1359, at the Corts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cervera">Cervera</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV</a> accepted a standing committee to collect the taxes voted by the Corts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Diputació del General</a>, later called the Generalitat, which grew into a powerful body defending the Principality’s constitutions. Barcelona’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-cent">Consell de Cent</a> and the other municipal councils enjoyed wide autonomy. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a> in 1412 brought the Castilian Trastámara dynasty to the throne, conflict between king and institutions grew, and the Constitution of Observance of 1481 bound royal officials to respect Catalan law. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> the kings, usually absent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, governed through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> in Barcelona, and the Corts met rarely. These institutions survived until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta</a> decree of 1716.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/consulate-of-the-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/consulate-of-the-sea.jpg" alt="Consulate of the Sea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/consulate-of-the-sea">Consulate of the Sea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Llotja_de_la_Seda_(52042949244).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kent Wang from Barcelona, Spain, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Principality of Catalonia was the commercial and maritime engine of the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, whose rule spread through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearics</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and, briefly, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Through that expansion Catalan language and law reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, the Balearic Islands and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alghero">Alghero</a> in Sardinia, where a Catalan dialect is still spoken. Its maritime code, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consulate-of-the-sea">Consolat de Mar</a></em>, influenced commercial law across the western Mediterranean, and its consuls and merchants created one of the great trading networks of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Catalan was a major literary language: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-llull">Ramon Llull</a> wrote philosophy and fiction in it, the chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon">James I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernat-desclot">Bernat Desclot</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-muntaner">Ramon Muntaner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV</a> are major works of medieval historiography, and the wider Catalan-speaking world produced the poetry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ausias-march">Ausiàs March</a> and the chivalric novel <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirant-lo-blanc">Tirant lo Blanc</a></em>. The Principality’s tradition of representative assemblies, contractual government and a standing fiscal committee made it one of the earliest parliamentary polities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its loss of self-government in 1714–1716 became central to modern Catalan identity: the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> is commemorated on 11 September, Catalonia’s national day, and the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat</a> was revived for the autonomous government of the twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/corpus-de-sang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corpus-de-sang.jpg" alt="Corpus de Sang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/corpus-de-sang">Corpus de Sang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Els_segadors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hermenegildo Miralles - (print in the public domain... is more than 100 years old &amp;amp; author died more then 70 year..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Catalonia’s relative decline began with the plagues of the mid-fourteenth century and the crises of the fifteenth, when population loss, bank failures, peasant revolt and the civil war of 1462–1472 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-aragon">John II</a> weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and its trade. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> the Principality was a peripheral part of a monarchy centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and it resisted royal demands for men and money. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspar-de-guzman-count-duke-of-olivares">Olivares</a> tried to make it contribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-arms">Union of Arms</a> and quartered troops there during the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, revolt broke out: on 7 June 1640, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-de-sang">Corpus de Sang</a>, reapers killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmau-de-queralt-count-of-santa-coloma">Count of Santa Coloma</a>. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pau-claris">Pau Claris</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat</a> proclaimed a short-lived Catalan Republic in January 1641 and then accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII of France</a> as count of Barcelona. Barcelona surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-spain">Philip IV</a>’s forces in 1652, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-the-pyrenees">Treaty of the Pyrenees</a> in 1659 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cerdanya">Cerdanya</a> were ceded to France. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> Catalonia backed the Habsburg claimant Archduke Charles, but after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> left it alone against Philip V, Barcelona fell in 1714. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta</a> decree of 16 January 1716 abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-courts">Corts</a>, the Generalitat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-cent">Consell de Cent</a>, and Catalonia was ruled as a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Principality of Catalonia, with its parliament, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalitat-de-catalunya">Generalitat</a> and merchant capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> expansion of the Crown of Aragon, developed one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s earliest traditions of contractual government and Catalan literature, and its loss of self-government in 1714–1716 still shapes Catalan identity.</em></p>

